Article:

Most refineries contain thousands of solenoid valves. The majority of applications are for ON/OFF (two-position) compared to other types of control valves that are able to provide a continuous range from 0% to 100% open.

🧲 In Physics, a solenoid is a coil of wire that produces a magnetic field when current is passed through it.

The typical engineering definition of a solenoid takes the same coil of wire, but adds a magnetic rod positioned inside of the coil.

When current is passed through the wire, the rod is pulled into the coil. When the current is stopped, the rod returns to the starting position.

The movement of the rod is how solenoids convert electrical energy to linear energy.

Solenoid valves use this energy conversion to open and close.

GO DEEPER

✏️ Alternate names for the magnetic rod are “plunger” and “armature”.

✏️ Some benefits of solenoid valves are very quick reaction times, high-reliability, and long service life.

✏️ A non-valve solenoid application found in refineries is the solenoid switch that is used in electrical relays. This isolates the operator from high-power circuits. (I wrote a previous LinkedIn post about switches and relays that I’ll link to in the comments)

